## VULNERABILITY ROOT CAUSE
  ### Vulnerability type and sub-pattern analysis
  - **Type:** stack-buffer-overflow
  - **Sub-pattern:** **unbounded `%[]` conversion in `fscanf()` into a fixed-size stack buffer**
  - **Vulnerable buffers (stack):**
    - `char temp[32];`
    - `char signtmp[32];`
  - **Root cause:** the `fscanf()` format string uses scansets (`%[ \t]` and `%[ \t+-]`) **without a field width**, so `fscanf()` will keep writing matching characters until a non-matching character is encountered, regardless of the destination buffer size.
  ### Forward analysis (reachability from entry point)
  **Entry:** `main()` in `src/bin/jp2/opj_compress.c:1831`
  At `src/bin/jp2/opj_compress.c:1831`, the program dispatches based on the detected/selected input format:
  ```c
  case PGX_DFMT:
      image = pgxtoimage(parameters.infile, &parameters);
      if (!image) { ... }
      break;
  ```
  So to reach the crash:
  1. The user supplies `-i <path/to/testcase>` and the tool determines (via command-line parsing / extension) that the input is **PGX** (`parameters.decod_format == PGX_DFMT`).
  2. `pgxtoimage()` is called with the attacker-controlled file path.
  **Input format requirement to reach the vulnerable parsing:**
  - The file must be opened successfully.
  - The file must begin with something that makes `fscanf()` attempt to parse a PGX header starting with `"PG..."` (because the format string begins with literal `"PG"`).
  ### Backward analysis (from crash point to explain the overflow)
  **Crash site:** `src/bin/jp2/convert.c:1188` inside `pgxtoimage()`
  Relevant code:
  ```c
  char signtmp[32];
  char temp[32];
  if (fscanf(f, "PG%[ \t]%c%c%[ \t+-]%d%[ \t]%d%[ \t]%d",
             temp, &endian1, &endian2, signtmp, &prec, temp, &w, temp, &h) != 9) {
      ...
  }
  ```
  #### Why this overflows the stack
  - `%[ \t]` reads a *sequence* of spaces/tabs into `temp`.
  - `%[ \t+-]` reads a *sequence* of spaces/tabs/`+`/`-` into `signtmp`.
  - Those conversions **do not specify a maximum width** (e.g., `%31[ \t]`), so if the input contains:
    - more than 31 spaces/tabs after `"PG"`, or
    - more than 31 characters from the set `[ \t+-]` before the precision integer,
    then `fscanf()` will write past the end of `temp[32]` or `signtmp[32]`.
  This is a classic C pitfall: **scanset conversions must always be width-limited** when writing into fixed-size buffers.
  #### Why this is attacker-controlled
  The attacker controls the PGX header text. For example, the header structure being parsed is effectively:
  ```
  "PG" + <whitespace> + <endian1><endian2> + <sign/whitespace> + <prec> + <whitespace> + <w> + <whitespace> + <h>
  ```
  Because `<whitespace>` and `<sign/whitespace>` are read using unbounded scansets, an attacker can insert a long run of matching characters to overflow.
  ### Triggering conditions and key constraints
  To trigger the overflow reliably:
  - The file must start with `"PG"` so the literal match succeeds.
  - Immediately after `"PG"`, provide **a long run of spaces/tabs** (to overflow `temp` via the first `%[ \t]`), *or* after the two endian characters provide **a long run of characters from `[ \t+-]`** (to overflow `signtmp`).
  - The rest of the header can be arranged so that `fscanf()` continues parsing (often it will still return 9 if the overwritten stack doesn’t immediately break parsing), but the overflow itself occurs during the scanset write.
  ### Vulnerability behavior description
  - The overflow occurs **before** any meaningful validation of `w`, `h`, `prec`, or endianness.
  - The overwritten stack memory can corrupt adjacent local variables, saved registers, or return address (depending on compiler/ABI/stack layout), leading to:
    - immediate crash (ASan detects stack-buffer-overflow),
    - or potentially control-flow corruption in non-sanitized builds.
  ### Relevant insecure code snippet (core issue)
  `src/bin/jp2/convert.c`:
  ```c
  char signtmp[32];
  char temp[32];
  fscanf(f, "PG%[ \t]%c%c%[ \t+-]%d%[ \t]%d%[ \t]%d",
         temp, &endian1, &endian2, signtmp, &prec, temp, &w, temp, &h);
  ```
  **What’s missing:** width specifiers:
  - Should be something like `"PG%31[ \t]%c%c%31[ \t+-]%d%31[ \t]%d%31[ \t]%d"` (or better, avoid `fscanf` for this and parse with bounded reads).
  ### Root cause summary
  The root cause is **unsafe parsing of the PGX header using `fscanf()` scansets without field widths**, causing **unbounded writes into fixed-size stack arrays** (`temp[32]`, `signtmp[32]`). The vulnerability is localized at the crash site in `pgxtoimage()`; upstream code only needs to route the input file to the PGX decoder to reach it.
